  {
    "channel": "final",
    "text": "I’d challenge “put everything into a perfect week.” If the demands exceed the available time, a useful planner must reveal the shortfall and help make tradeoffs. A packed calendar can conceal an impossible workload.\n\nI recommend separating three things:\n\n- **Fixed timing:** commitments whose time cannot reasonably move, such as a flight or a pickup with no alternative arrangement.\n- **Protected needs:** things that need enough space, such as sleep, caregiving or recovery, even when some timing is flexible.\n- **Preferences:** desirable placements, such as exercising at 7 a.m. rather than later.\n\nA deadline illustrates the distinction: Friday’s submission may be fixed; Tuesday’s writing session may be movable. Making both fixed unnecessarily reduces the planner’s options. But calling everything movable could erase the personal needs the week must support.\n\n**Should we use this narrow definition of “fixed”—an exact time that cannot reasonably move—or is there a commitment you’d want treated as fixed despite having movable timing?** One example you’re unsure about would help us test that rule."
  }
